        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "4709849a-e8a4-54da-b8a2-ba9e2bd2bf6d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lincoln Steffens, Monique Wittig, Doc Ames\n- Works: McClure's, Tweed Days in St. Louis, The Shame of the Cities, L’Opoponax\n- Topics: Organized crime in Minneapolis, feminism, gender roles\n\nFacts:\n- Lincoln Steffens was born on April 6, 1866, and he was a New York reporter.\n- In 1903, organized crime in Minneapolis first attracted national attention when Doc Ames was exposed by Lincoln Steffens in The Shame of the Cities.\n- Lincoln Steffens launched a series of articles in McClure's called Tweed Days in St. Louis, and those articles were later published together as the book The Shame of the Cities.\n- Monique Wittig was born on July 13, 1935, and she was a French author and feminist theorist.\n- L’Opoponax is a 1964 novel by Monique Wittig.\n- Monique Wittig wrote about overcoming socially enforced gender roles and coined the phrase \"heterosexual contract.\"\n- Monique Wittig died on January 3, 2003.\n- Lincoln Steffens died on August 9, 1936.\n- Doc Ames lived from 1842 to 1911.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
